['Here is an example of how you might implement this in C using dlopen/dlsym to load shared libraries dynamically. This code does not include error handling, memory management or optimization for performance under high load as these are complex topics that would require a more detailed understanding of the specific requirements and constraints of your system.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

typedef struct {
    const char* name;
    void* (*hash_func)(const unsigned char*, size_t);
} HashAlgorithm;

HashAlgorithm* load_algorithms() {
    // List of supported algorithms
    static const char* algorithm_names[] = {"md5", "sha1", "sha256", NULL};

    // Allocate memory for the list of pointers to HashAlgorithm structs
    int num_algorithms = 0;
    while (algorithm_names[num_algorithms] != NULL) {
        num_algorithms++;
    }
    HashAlgorithm* algorithms = malloc(sizeof(HashAlgorithm) * num_algorithms);

    // Load each algorithm from a shared library
    for (int i = 0; i < num_algorithms; i++) {
        const char* name = algorithm_names[i];

        // Open the shared library
        void* handle = dlopen(name, RTLD_LAZY);
        if (!handle) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Failed to load %s: %s\n", name, dlerror());
            continue;
        }

        // Get the hash function from the library
        void* (*hash_func)(const unsigned char*, size_t);
        *(void **) (&hash_func) = dlsym(handle, "hash");
        if (!hash_func) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Failed to find hash function in %s: %s\n", name, dlerror());
            continue;
        }

        // Add the algorithm to the list
        algorithms[i].name = name;
        algorithms[i].hash_func = hash_func;
    }

    return algorithms;
}
```
This code assumes that each shared library exports a function named "hash" which is used as the implementation of the hash algorithm. The actual names and locations of these libraries would need to be determined based on your specific requirements and constraints, such as platform differences or user-specified paths. 

Please note that this is just an example and may not work in a real system without modifications. It also does not include error handling for memory allocation failures, which you should add if necessary.
