['Here is a simple implementation of your request using SHA-256 hash algorithm. This code does not handle exceptions for simplicity. In a real world scenario, you should add proper exception handling. 

```java
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.security.SecureRandom;
import java.util.Base64;

public class HashGenerator {
    private static final SecureRandom secureRandom = new SecureRandom();

    public String generateHash(String input, byte[] salt) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        if (input == null || input.length() > 4096) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input string is too long");
        }

        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
        md.update(salt);
        byte[] hashedPasswordBytes = md.digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));

        return Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(hashedPasswordBytes);
    }

    public byte[] generateSalt() {
        byte[] salt = new byte[16];
        secureRandom.nextBytes(salt);
        return salt;
    }
}
```

This code uses SHA-256 for hashing, which is a widely used hash function that provides good security but can be slow on some systems. The salt is generated using SecureRandom, which is designed to generate random numbers suitable for cryptographic use. 

The length of the salt is fixed at 16 bytes (128 bits), which is generally considered sufficient for most purposes. However, you could adjust this depending on your specific needs. 

This code does not handle the case where the input string exceeds 4096 characters combined with the salt. In a real-world scenario, you would need to add appropriate error handling for this situation. 

The performance of SHA-256 can be affected by the specific hardware and software environment in which it is run. On modern systems, it should provide adequate security while being relatively fast. However, if speed is a critical requirement, you may want to consider using a different hash function or algorithm that provides better performance at the cost of some security.
