['This is a complex task that requires a deep understanding of Java NIO Selectors, networking, concurrency, and performance optimization. Here's a simplified example of how you might start implementing such a service using Netty, a high-performance event-driven network application framework. 

```java
import io.netty.bootstrap.Bootstrap;
import io.netty.channel.*;
import io.netty.channel.nio.NioEventLoopGroup;
import io.netty.channel.socket.nio.NioSocketChannel;
import io.netty.handler.codec.string.StringDecoder;
import io.netty.handler.codec.string.StringEncoder;
import io.netty.util.CharsetUtil;

public class NettyClient {
    private final String host;
    private final int port;
    private EventLoopGroup group;
    private Bootstrap bootstrap;
    private Channel channel;

    public NettyClient(String host, int port) {
        this.host = host;
        this.port = port;
    }

    public void start() throws Exception {
        group = new NioEventLoopGroup();
        bootstrap = new Bootstrap();
        bootstrap.group(group)
                .channel(NioSocketChannel.class)
                .handler(new ChannelInitializer<Channel>() {
                    @Override
                    protected void initChannel(Channel ch) throws Exception {
                        ChannelPipeline pipeline = ch.pipeline();
                        pipeline.addLast("decoder", new StringDecoder(CharsetUtil.UTF_8));
                        pipeline.addLast("encoder", new StringEncoder(CharsetUtil.UTF_8));
                    }
                });
        channel = bootstrap.connect(host, port).sync().channel();
    }

    public void stop() {
        group.shutdownGracefully();
    }
}
```
This is a basic Netty client that connects to a remote host and port. It uses NIO for non-blocking I/O operations, which can significantly improve performance when dealing with many connections. 

For load balancing across multiple addresses, you could use a round-robin strategy or implement some form of dynamic load balancing based on the current connection health and load. This would involve maintaining a list of available remote addresses and distributing incoming requests among them in a way that maximizes resource utilization while minimizing latency.

For handling transient failures, you could use Netty's built-in retry logic or implement your own custom backoff strategy. You might also want to integrate an external monitoring tool like Prometheus for tracking connection health and performance metrics.

To ensure resilience against network disruptions, you should handle exceptions properly and implement failover and retry mechanisms. This could involve retrying failed requests automatically or routing traffic to a backup server when the primary one fails.

The choice of connection pool size will depend on your specific requirements and constraints. A larger pool can improve performance by reducing the overhead of establishing new connections, but it also consumes more memory. You should balance this against response time and overall system health. 

Finally, remember to optimize for latency while minimizing resource consumption. This might involve tuning Netty's event loop group size, buffer sizes, and other settings to achieve maximum efficiency without consuming excessive resources.
