['Here is an example of how you might implement this function using PHP with PDO for database operations and Monolog for logging. This code assumes that you have a User class or similar to represent your users and their roles/departments, and that you're using a MySQL-compatible database. 

```php
<?php
use Monolog\Logger;
use Monolog\Handler\StreamHandler;

class BatchUpdate {
    private $dbh;
    private $logger;

    public function __construct(PDO $dbh) {
        $this->dbh = $dbh;
        $this->logger = new Logger('BatchUpdate');
        $this->logger->pushHandler(new StreamHandler(__DIR__.'/logs/batch_update.log', Logger::DEBUG));
    }

    public function updateUsers(array $users) {
        try {
            $this->dbh->beginTransaction();

            foreach ($users as $user) {
                $stmt = $this->dbh->prepare("UPDATE users SET role = ?, department = ? WHERE id = ?");
                if (!$stmt->execute([$user->getRole(), $user->getDepartment(), $user->getId()])) {
                    $this->logger->error('Failed to update user', ['id' => $user->getId()]);
                    continue;
                }
                $this->logger->info('Updated user', ['id' => $user->getId()]);
            }

            $this->dbh->commit();
        } catch (PDOException $e) {
            $this->dbh->rollBack();
            $this->logger->error('Transaction failed: ' . $e->getMessage());
        }
    }
}
?>
```
This code uses a transaction to ensure that all updates are performed or none of them are, which can help maintain data integrity. It also logs each operation's progress and outcome for operational auditing. The function is modular and can be easily integrated with external APIs or third-party services by adding additional methods that interact with these services.
']

