['Here is a Java method that meets your requirements:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.security.*;

public class Deserializer {
    public static Collection<SerializableObject> deserializeObjects(byte[] data) throws RuntimeException {
        List<SerializableObject> objects = new ArrayList<>();

        try (ByteArrayInputStream bais = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
             ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bais)) {
            while (true) {
                SerializableObject obj = (SerializableObject) ois.readObject();

                // Verify the integrity of the serialized data
                MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
                byte[] checksumData = md.digest(obj.toString().getBytes());
                if (!Arrays.equals(checksumData, obj.getChecksum())) {
                    throw new RuntimeException("Deserialization failed: Checksums do not match.");
                }

                objects.add(obj);
            }
        } catch (EOFException e) {
            // End of file reached, all objects have been deserialized
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException | NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Deserialization failed: " + e.getMessage(), e);
        }

        return objects;
    }
}
```
This method uses `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize the byte array into a collection of `SerializableObject` instances. It calculates a checksum from each object's state after deserialization and compares it with the embedded checksum within the byte array. If they do not match, an exception is thrown.

The method handles potential deserialization failures gracefully by catching `IOException`, `ClassNotFoundException`, and `NoSuchAlgorithmException` exceptions and re-throwing them as a `RuntimeException` with a descriptive message. This ensures that the application can continue running even if some objects in the byte array cannot be deserialized.

The method also handles the end of file (EOF) exception, which is thrown when all objects have been successfully deserialized from the byte array. In this case, the method simply returns the collection of deserialized objects.

Please note that you need to implement `SerializableObject` interface and provide a `getChecksum()` method in your class. This method should return the checksum embedded within the serialized data. The checksum can be calculated using any hash function, such as SHA-256.
']

