['Here is a basic implementation of the `insertAfterSafe` function. It uses a mutex to ensure thread safety, reuses nodes when possible, and handles large volumes of data efficiently by using a pool allocator for node allocation. 

Please note that this code does not include error checking or exception handling, which would be necessary in a production environment. Also, it assumes that the `Node` class has been defined elsewhere in your program.

```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

class Node {
public:
    std::string str;
    int value;
    std::shared_ptr<Node> next, prev;

    Node(const std::string& s, int v) : str(s), value(v) {}
};

std::mutex mtx; // Mutex for thread safety

// Pool allocator for node reuse
class NodePool {
public:
    static std::shared_ptr<Node> get() {
        if (pool.empty()) return std::make_shared<Node>();

        auto node = pool.back();
        pool.pop_back();
        return node;
    }

    static void put(std::shared_ptr<Node> node) {
        pool.push_back(node);
    }
private:
    static std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Node>> pool;
};

// Initialize the pool allocator
std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Node>> NodePool::pool = {};

void insertAfterSafe(std::shared_ptr<Node> iterator, const std::string& str, int value) {
    // Lock to ensure thread safety
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    auto newNode = NodePool::get();
    newNode->str = str;
    newNode->value = value;

    if (iterator->next) {
        iterator->next->prev = newNode;
        newNode->next = iterator->next;
    }

    iterator->next = newNode;
    newNode->prev = iterator;
}
```
This code assumes that the doubly linked list is implemented as a circular list with a sentinel node. If your implementation differs, you may need to adjust this code accordingly. 

Also note that this code does not handle concurrent modifications from multiple threads. Depending on your specific use case and requirements, you might want to consider using a lock-free data structure or other synchronization mechanisms for better performance under high contention scenarios.
']

