['Here is a basic implementation of the task in C language using pthreads for concurrency, mutexes for thread safety, and linked list operations. This code does not handle all constraints mentioned above but provides a starting point. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct Measurement {
    float value;
    time_t timestamp;
    struct Measurement *next;
} Measurement;

Measurement* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void addMeasurement(float value, time_t timestamp) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Measurement *newNode = malloc(sizeof(Measurement));
    newNode->value = value;
    newNode->timestamp = timestamp;
    newNode->next = NULL;

    if (head == NULL) {
        head = newNode;
    } else {
        Measurement *current = head;
        while(current->next != NULL && current->next->timestamp < timestamp) {
            current = current->next;
        }

        if (current->timestamp == timestamp) {
            free(newNode);
            return;
        } else {
            newNode->next = current->next;
            current->next = newNode;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code creates a linked list of `Measurement` structures. Each measurement has a value, timestamp and pointer to the next node in the list. The function `addMeasurement()` adds a new measurement to the list while ensuring that no two consecutive measurements have the same timestamp. It also uses mutexes for thread safety.

Please note that this is a basic implementation and does not handle all constraints mentioned above, such as memory management, performance constraints, scalability, data integrity, resource utilization etc. For these, you would need to implement additional logic and possibly use more advanced data structures or algorithms. Also, testing under different load conditions should be done using appropriate tools and techniques.
