["Here is an example of how you might implement this in JavaScript using the `fetch` API and promises:

```javascript
class FileManager {
  constructor(config) {
    this.cache = {};
    this.fileQueue = [];
    this.config = config;
  }

  loadFile(path, type) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      if (this.cache[path]) {
        resolve(this.cache[path]);
      } else {
        fetch(path)
          .then(response => response.text())
          .then(data => {
            this.cache[path] = data;
            resolve(data);
          })
          .catch(error => reject(`Error loading file: ${error}`));
      }
    });
  }

  executeFile(path, type) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      this.loadFile(path, type)
        .then(data => {
          try {
            let script = document.createElement('script');
            script.textContent = data;
            document.head.appendChild(script);
            resolve();
          } catch (error) {
            reject(`Error executing file: ${error}`);
          }
        })
        .catch(error => reject(error));
    });
  }

  loadAndExecuteFiles() {
    this.config.files.forEach((file, index) => {
      let promise = this.executeFile(file.path, file.type);
      if (index > 0 && this.config.order === 'sequential') {
        this.fileQueue.push(promise);
      } else {
        promise.then(() => delete this.cache[file.path]);
      }
    });

    return Promise.allSettled(this.fileQueue)
      .then(() => console.log('All files loaded and executed'))
      .catch(error => console.error(`Error loading or executing files: ${error}`));
  }
}
```

This code creates a `FileManager` class that can load and execute JavaScript files dynamically within the context of a web application. It uses the `fetch` API to asynchronously load files, caching them for future use. The `executeFile` method takes care of executing the file content in the correct context (e.g., global or module scope).

The `loadAndExecuteFiles` method is responsible for coordinating the loading and execution of multiple files based on a configuration object. It supports both sequential and concurrent execution orders, with each file's execution depending on the successful completion of the previous one.

This code also handles potential operational constraints such as network latency or server-side processing delays by using promises to handle asynchronous operations. If a file fails to load or execute due to an error, the promise is rejected and the error message is logged for debugging purposes. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and might need adjustments based on your specific requirements and constraints. For instance, you may want to add more sophisticated caching mechanisms (e.g., with expiration times), handle different file types differently, or provide better feedback to the user in case of errors.
