['Here is a basic example of how you might implement this in C using POSIX threads (pthreads) for thread safety and OpenMP for parallelization. Note that this is a simplified version and does not include all the features mentioned, such as error handling or external dependencies. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <omp.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int id;
    double timestamp;
    char status;
} Record;

Record* records = NULL;
int record_count = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

void init_records(int count, int min, int max) {
    srand(time(NULL)); // seed the random number generator

    if (count > 10000 || count < 0) {
        printf("Invalid record count\n");
        return;
    }

    records = malloc(sizeof(Record) * count);
    record_count = count;

    #pragma omp parallel for num_threads(4) // use 4 threads to populate the array in parallel
    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

        records[i].id = rand() % (max - min + 1) + min; // generate a random number within the specified range
        records[i].timestamp = time(NULL); // get the current time in seconds since the epoch
        records[i].status = 'I'; // set the status to an indicator

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
}

void free_records() {
    if (records != NULL) {
        free(records);
        records = NULL;
    }
}
```
This code uses OpenMP to parallelize the record initialization process, which can significantly improve performance for large numbers of records. It also uses a mutex lock to ensure thread safety when accessing and modifying the `records` array. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include all the features mentioned in your task description. For instance, it doesn't handle errors or external dependencies, nor does it provide logging mechanisms for debugging purposes. Also, it assumes that the random number generator and time functions are thread-safe, which may not be true on some platforms or with certain compilers/libraries. 

