["Here's a JavaScript implementation of your requirements using recursion for traversing the DOM tree and promises for handling external JSON configurations:

```javascript
async function processDOMElements(node, sizeLimit, filterCallback, includeWhitespace = false, configFilePath) {
    let totalSize = 0;
    let textContent = '';

    if (configFilePath) {
        try {
            const response = await fetch(configFilePath);
            const data = await response.json();
            sizeLimit = data.sizeLimit || sizeLimit;
        } catch (error) {
            console.log('Error loading JSON configuration:', error);
        }
    }

    if (!filterCallback(node)) return null;

    for (let child of node.childNodes) {
        switch (child.nodeType) {
            case Node.TEXT_NODE:
                textContent += includeWhitespace ? child.textContent : child.textContent.trim();
                break;
            case Node.ELEMENT_NODE:
                const processedChild = await processDOMElements(child, sizeLimit, filterCallback, includeWhitespace, configFilePath);
                if (processedChild !== null) {
                    textContent += processedChild;
                } else {
                    return null;
                }
        }
    }

    totalSize = textContent.length;

    if (totalSize > sizeLimit) {
        return null;
    } else {
        node.textContent = '/' + textContent;
        return node.outerHTML;
    }
}
```
This function uses the Fetch API to load a JSON configuration file, and it handles potential loading errors by logging them to the console. It also uses recursion to traverse the DOM tree, applying the logic of `limitExceeded` to each element's text content. The function respects the callback function for filtering elements based on specific attributes.
