["Here's an example of how you might implement this in C. This code does not include all the details you requested (like thread safety, error handling for memory allocation failures and batch processing), but it should give you a good starting point. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
    char* name;
} record;

typedef struct Node {
    // attributes of the node
    struct Node *children;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

// Callback function type definition
typedef int (*CriteriaFunction)(Node*);

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

record* traverse(Node* root, CriteriaFunction criteria) {
    record *records = malloc(sizeof(record)); // Initialize memory for records
    int count = 0;

    if (root == NULL || criteria(root)) {
        return records;
    }

    Node* child = root->children;
    while (child != NULL) {
        record *new_records = traverse(child, criteria);

        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Lock to ensure thread safety
        int new_count = 0;
        while (new_records[new_count].name != NULL) {
            new_count++;
        }

        records = realloc(records, sizeof(record) * (count + new_count + 1)); // Reallocate memory for more records
        memcpy(&records[count], new_records, sizeof(record) * new_count); // Copy new records to the main array

        count += new_count;
        free(new_records); // Free the temporary array
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock after critical section

        child = child->next;
    }

    return records;
}
```
This code uses a recursive function to traverse the DOM tree. It locks a mutex before entering the critical section (where shared resources are accessed), and unlocks it afterwards. This ensures that only one thread can access the shared resource at a time, preventing race conditions. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include all the details you requested. For instance, error handling for memory allocation failures, batch processing or asynchronous operations are not included in this code. Also, it's important to remember that C doesn't have built-in garbage collection like some other languages do, so manual memory management is crucial when working with C.
"]

