['Here is a C code snippet implementing the `initializeDataStructures` function as per your requirements. This code uses POSIX threads (pthreads) to ensure thread safety during memory allocation and deallocation. It also checks if the allocated memory is page-aligned, which can help optimize cache performance.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
} Record;

static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void initializeDataStructures(long* offsets, size_t offsetSize, 
                              int* keys, size_t keySize, 
                              Record** records, size_t recordSize) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    // Allocate memory for arrays
    offsets = malloc(offsetSize * sizeof(*offsets));
    keys = malloc(keySize * sizeof(*keys));
    posix_memalign((void**)records, 16, recordSize * sizeof(Record));

    // Check if memory is page-aligned
    if ((uintptr_t)*records % 4096 != 0) {
        printf("Memory not aligned to a page boundary\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    srand(time(NULL)); // Seed the random number generator

    // Fill records array with random int values within a specified range
    for (size_t i = 0; i < recordSize; ++i) {
        (*records)[i].value = rand() % 100 + 1; // Change this to your desired range
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void cleanup(long* offsets, int* keys, Record** records) {
    free(offsets);
    free(keys);
    free(*records);
}
```

This code assumes that the system's page size is 4096 bytes. If this is not the case, you may need to adjust the check for alignment accordingly. Also note that `posix_memalign` is used instead of `malloc` to ensure proper alignment. This function returns a pointer to aligned memory suitable for any object whose size is a multiple of the alignment parameter.

The cleanup routine frees all allocated memory upon function exit, regardless of success or failure, to prevent memory leaks. The use of local variables for temporary storage instead of the stack minimizes the risk of stack overflow during the cleanup process. 

This code also uses a mutex lock to ensure thread safety during memory allocation and deallocation. This is important in multi-threaded environments where multiple threads may be accessing or modifying the same data structures simultaneously.
